<h2 style="text-align: left;">2. Scope and limitations</h2>
<p style="text-align: left;"><em>Elogic</em> is opinionated. The natural deduction system is Kalish-Montague style in Fitch format, aligned specifically with Parsons&#8217;s <a href="https://logiclx.humnet.ucla.edu/Logic/Documents/CORE/LogicText%20Chap%200%20Aug%202013.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>An Exposition of Symbolic Logic</em></a>. The notation, rule names, and proof conventions match that presentation. This is designed for courses using that text or something close to it, not as a general-purpose tool configurable for arbitrary proof systems or notations. (This mostly applies to the derivation exercises, not so much the symbolizations, truth tables, etc.)</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">If you need something more flexible, various notations, various proof systems, <a href="https://carnap.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carnap.io</a> is the project to look at. <em>Elogic</em> does one thing and tries to do it well.</p>
<h2 style="text-align: left;">3. It&#8217;s free</h2>
<p style="text-align: left;"><em>Elogic</em> used to be behind course join codes with licensing fees, to cover cloud hosting. That&#8217;s over. It&#8217;s now free educational software, no sign-up required. Three ways in:</p>
<ul>
<li style="text-align: left;">Try immediately: click &#8220;Try without an account.&#8221; No sign-up, nothing. Progress saves in your browser.</li>
<li style="text-align: left;">Sign up free: create an account and your work syncs across devices.</li>
<li style="text-align: left;">Run a course: instructors get an admin dashboard with student join codes, a roster, per-assignment progress stats, grade exports, and the ability to view any student&#8217;s work. Also free.</li>
</ul>
<h2 style="text-align: left;">4. Getting started</h2>
<p style="text-align: left;">Go to <a href="https://elogic.land/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">elogic.land</a>, click &#8220;Try without an account,&#8221; pick a problem, work it, hit Check. See the help guide for how to use it: <a href="https://elogic.land/help" target="_blank" rel="noopener">elogic.land/help</a></p>
<p style="text-align: left;">If you&#8217;re an instructor interested in using it for a class, email me and I&#8217;ll set you up with a course.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">This was built because I needed it and couldn&#8217;t find it. The intention is for <em>Elogic</em> to stay free permanently. There is no secret upsell, no premium tier. I just have a thing that I and others have found useful for teaching logic, and it seemed wrong not to share it. So I&#8217;ve opened it up. Feedback (to <a href="mailto:brian.rabern@gmail.com">brian.rabern@gmail.com</a>) is always welcome: what works, what doesn&#8217;t, what&#8217;s confusing.</p><p>The post <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/07/13/free-web-based-logic-software-guest-post/">Free Web-Based Logic Software (guest post)</a> first appeared on <a href="https://dailynous.com">Daily Nous</a>.</p>